'''Khazar''', also known as '''Khazaric''', was a [[Turkic languages|Turkic]] dialect group spoken by the [[Khazars]], a group of semi-[[nomadic]] [[Turkic peoples]] originating from [[Central Asia]]. There are few written records of the language and its features and characteristics are unknown. It is believed to have gradually become extinct by the 13th century AD as its speakers assimilated into neighboring Turkic-speaking populations.

As the extant corpus of Khazar is extremely limited, consisting of two nouns, a conjugated verb, and a few proper names, its exact genealogical position within the Turkic language family remains unresolved. Some scholars believe that it belongs to the [[Oghuric languages|Oghuric]] branch of the Turkic language family, while others place it in the [[Common Turkic]] branch.

==Classification==

There are many problems with exact classification of the Khazar language. One of the basic issues is the vague nature of the name ''Khazar'' itself. It has not yet been determined whether it refers to a specific Turkic tribe, or if it had a political and geographical origin that was not ethnolinguistic.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=224}} The Khazar realm was a multilingual and multicultural state, with speakers of Iranian, Finno-Ugric, Slavic, and North Caucasian languages among its inhabitants.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=151}}{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=224}}{{sfn|Golden|1992|p=235}} The Turkic tribes probably spoke a number of Turkic languages.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=151, 224}} Scholars considered possible that the term ''Khazar'' denoted one or even several languages; however, the extent to which an "official" Khazar language was used in the Khazar state is unknown.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=225}}

Contemporary accounts are unclear on Khazar's linguistic affiliation. The tenth-century traveler and geographer [[Estakhri|al-Istakhri]] wrote two conflicting notices: "the language of the Khazars is different than the language of the Turks and the Persians, nor does a tongue of (any) group of humanity have anything in common with it, and the language of the [[Bulgars]] is like the language of the Khazars but the [[Burtas]] have another language."{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=225}} Al-Istakhri mentioned that the population of [[Derbent|Darband]] spoke Khazar along with other languages of their mountains.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=226}} [[Al-Masudi]] (896 – 956) listed the Khazars among the types of Turks and noted that they are called ''[[Sabir people|Sabir]]'' in Turkic and ''Xazar'' in Persian.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=225}} [[Al-Biruni]] (973 – 1050), while discussing the Volga Bulgars and Sawars (Sabirs), noted their language was a "mixture of Turkic and Khazar."{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=226}}{{sfn|Golden|1992|p=235}} [[Al-Muqaddasi]] (<abbr>c.</abbr> 945/946 – 991) described the Khazar language as "very incomprehensible."{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=226}} [[Ibn Hawqal]], who travelled during the years 943 to 969 AD,<ref name="Ludwig 2009">[[Ludwig W. Adamec]] (2009), ''Historical Dictionary of Islam'', p.137. Scarecrow Press. {{ISBN|0810861615}}.</ref> wrote that "the Bulgar language resembles that of the Khazars".<ref>{{cite book | last=Brook | first=Kevin Alan | title=The Jews of Khazaria |publisher=Jason Aronson | publication-place=Northvale, NJ | date=1999 | isbn=0-7657-6032-0 | oclc=39655011 | page=63}}</ref>{{sfn|Erdal|2007|p=83}}

Historian [[Peter Benjamin Golden|Peter B. Golden]] writes: "It is probable that even if 'Khazar' (or one of Khazaria's principal languages) did belong to some branch of Oğuro-Bulğaric, it was sufficiently different that people distinguished between the two." He contrasts this with the relative uniformity of Common Turkic, of which al-Istakhri wrote, "as for the Turks, all of them, from the [[Toquz Oghuz|Toquz Oğuz]], [[Yenisei Kyrgyz|Qırğız]], [[Kimek tribe|Kimek]], [[Oghuz Turks|Oğuz]], [[Karluks|Qarluq]], their language is one. They understand one another".{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=227}}

==Vocabulary==
The linguistic data on Khazar consists mostly of [[proper name]]s such as [[Royal title|titles]] ([[Bey|Beg]], [[Baliqchi|Bolušči]], [[Ishad]], [[Elteber|Il-teber/El-teber]], [[Khagan|Qağan]], [[Kündür|Kündü Qağan]], [[Jawyshyghr|Jâwšîġr]], [[Tarkhan|Tarxan]], [[Tudun]], [[Yabgu]], Yilig/Yelig), anthroponyms (Itaq), and toponyms ([[Sarkel|Sarkel/Šarkil]], [[Saqsin|Sarığšın/Sarığčın]]), mostly of Turkic origin.{{sfn|Golden|1992|p=234–235}}{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=227–239}} The interpretations do not indicate whether these are Common Turkic or Oghuric.{{sfn|Golden|2011|p=150}}{{sfn|Erdal|2007|p=94}}

Just two [[common noun]]s have been attested. The Arab historian [[Ibn A'tham al-Kufi]] records the name of a type of [[tent]] as ''alǰdāḏ'', whose first part is probably a cognate of eastern Old Turkic ''alaču'' 'tent'. A word for 'funeral feast' is recorded by the Byzantine historian [[Theophanes the Confessor|Theophanes]] in several forms: ''δοχήν'' ''dokhḗn'', ''δογήν'' ''dogḗn'', ''δογῆν'' ''dogên'', ''δουγήν'' ''dougén'', comparable with eastern Old Turkic ''yog'' (as well as with a term recorded by [[Menander Protector|Menandros]] as ''δογια'' ''dogia''). Other nouns have been proposed to be reflected in Khazar proper names, such as *''bulan'' 'elk', *''ït'' 'dog' in the personal names ''[[Bulan (Khazar)|Bulan]]'' and ''Itakh''.<ref>{{cite book|first1=András|last1=Róna-Tas|first2=Árpád|last2=Berta|title=West Old Turkic|volume=2|pages=1168–1172|year=2011|publisher=Harrassowitz|location=Wiesbaden}}</ref>

Khazar was stated by the 1986 ''[[Guinness World Records|Guinness Book of Records]]'' (following a claim by the ''[[Great Soviet Encyclopedia]]'') to have the "smallest literature" of any language, allegedly comprising only one attested word, ''oqurüm'', "I have read" (from the [[Kievan Letter]]).<ref>{{cite book |editor1-last=McWhirter |editor1-first=Norris |editor1-link=Norris McWhirter |title=Guinness Book of World Records |date=1986 |publisher=[[Sterling Publishing]] |location=New York |isbn=0-8069-0272-8 |page=136 |chapter-url=https://archive.org/details/guinnessbook00mcwh/page/136/mode/1up |access-date=2022-01-06 |chapter=The Arts and Entertainment: Language and Literature — Smallest Literature|chapter-url-access=limited}}</ref>

